<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Adiabene</h1><p class="st-meta">164 BCE – 379 CE (approx.) · capital Arbela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Parthian vassal kingdom centred on Arbela (Erbil) whose royal family converted to Judaism in the 1st century CE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Adiabene lay between the Great and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-zab">Lesser Zab</a> rivers east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, the heartland of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> control weakened in the 2nd century BCE, a local dynasty emerged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a>, becoming a client of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and at times of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> tells how Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helena-of-adiabene">Helena</a> and her son King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izates-ii">Izates II</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> in the 1st century CE; Helena sent food to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> during a famine and was buried in the so-called Tomb of the Kings there, and Adiabenian princes fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 66 to 70 CE. The population spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and practised a mix of Iranian, Mesopotamian, Jewish and, from early on, Christian faiths. Adiabene played a role in Parthian succession struggles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> briefly occupied Adiabene in 116 CE. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> it became a dependent kingdom and then a province, ending in the 4th century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Arbela</a> became a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-adiabene.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Adiabene" width="320" height="263"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Adiabene,_ca_37_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>164 BCE – 379 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arbela</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Izates II, Monobazus II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Judaism, Iranian religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Adiabene, Hadyab</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=108&amp;focus=kingdom-of-adiabene" aria-label="Kingdom of Adiabene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5788 872 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Adiabene lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6108 1128L6141 1232M6108 1128L5304 942M6108 1128L6357 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6141 1232h0M5304 942h0M6357 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6108 1128h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6108 1128h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=108&amp;focus=kingdom-of-adiabene">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

